00:15:07.790 --> 00:15:09.510
I was very focused on social innovation.

00:15:09.510 --> 00:15:13.026
So I and the GSB has mandatory trip, well,

00:15:13.026 --> 00:15:18.310
I don't know what to call it,
but I'm on the third trip abroad.

00:15:18.310 --> 00:15:22.393
It can be their business trip or
a social focus trip, and I actually took.

00:15:22.393 --> 00:15:28.560
So I went to India, that's in a mini
talk where I came out to my friends.

00:15:28.560 --> 00:15:32.321
I went to South India on health care,
South Africa and education,

00:15:32.321 --> 00:15:34.415
Kenya on social entrepreneurship.

00:15:34.415 --> 00:15:39.210
And then I did a nonprofit trip non
official to Burundi, Rwanda, and Uganda.

00:15:39.210 --> 00:15:43.817
And I think those trips are just amazing
the people you get to have conversations

00:15:43.817 --> 00:15:46.920
with the expansion of
a global perspective.

00:15:46.920 --> 00:15:49.680
And the friendships that you develop
from this trip is just amazing.

00:15:54.243 --> 00:15:57.687
Yeah, I'll try emphasize
the talk that as well.

00:15:57.687 --> 00:16:00.177
Definitely like hearing
other people's stories, but

00:16:00.177 --> 00:16:03.198
then going through the process of
the reflection that was kind of like

00:16:03.198 --> 00:16:06.233
a repeat of the what matters most
to you and why reflection process.

00:16:06.233 --> 00:16:08.290
It was just doubling down and

00:16:08.290 --> 00:16:11.993
doing it a bit more at least
that was the case for me.

00:16:11.993 --> 00:16:16.893
Yeah, and I think I spent the first
like year or so, not kind of I

00:16:16.893 --> 00:16:22.870
would say sharing all aspects of
my family background and life etc.

00:16:22.870 --> 00:16:27.408
And so obviously that's not a great
way to start out an experience, but

00:16:27.408 --> 00:16:31.230
I think the talk process kind
of helped me open up more.

00:16:31.230 --> 00:16:35.676
I think it's helped me realize some of the
reasons that I was maybe holding back were

00:16:35.676 --> 00:16:36.957
not really warranted.

00:16:36.957 --> 00:16:38.835
And that it was like to my benefit, so

00:16:38.835 --> 00:16:41.633
there's benefits to be more
transparent about that.

00:16:41.633 --> 00:16:45.968
Related to that my second year,
myself and a couple of my classmates,

00:16:45.968 --> 00:16:50.682
we helped start the first generation
low income diversity group at the GSB.

00:16:50.682 --> 00:16:55.620
And I mean, I think the thing I took away
from that is how many conversations I had

00:16:55.620 --> 00:16:59.086
with people about those things,
not just in my class but

00:16:59.086 --> 00:17:01.430
in the class below me as well.

00:17:01.430 --> 00:17:04.434
Of just like learning things
about others that otherwise,

00:17:04.434 --> 00:17:08.110
I would not have learned if I had not
put myself out there in a certain way.

00:17:08.110 --> 00:17:11.125
So I think that that's something
that I've taken away and

00:17:11.125 --> 00:17:12.780
continue to think about a lot.

00:17:12.780 --> 00:17:16.326
Obviously will certainly have
continued to have an impact in

00:17:16.326 --> 00:17:18.835
the way that I interact with other people.

00:17:22.162 --> 00:17:27.217
&gt;&gt; Yeah, thank you so much for
sharing that and I feel like you bring up,

00:17:27.217 --> 00:17:31.592
something that I'm really
curious to learn more about is.

00:17:31.592 --> 00:17:35.977
How did your GSB experience
impact the way you think about

00:17:35.977 --> 00:17:39.914
the role of identity and
diversity in leadership?

00:17:39.914 --> 00:17:43.422
I mean this panel is all
about diverse leaders and

00:17:43.422 --> 00:17:49.513
I'm curious how your perspectives on that
evolved because of your GSB experience.

00:17:55.681 --> 00:17:58.761
&gt;&gt; I could start, I mean, for
me it changed me completely.

00:17:58.761 --> 00:18:05.025
And I think it took me a while to
find an organization to work for,

00:18:05.025 --> 00:18:10.709
where my values, were aligned
in the way I learned to be and

00:18:10.709 --> 00:18:13.725
I learn to be myself at the GSB so

00:18:13.725 --> 00:18:18.494
it took me a while to get
to a place like Facebook.

00:18:18.494 --> 00:18:20.528
I was interim country director for
Facebook, Mexico, and

00:18:20.528 --> 00:18:22.399
I was interim country director for
when COVID hits, so

00:18:22.399 --> 00:18:24.690
I was in charge of
managing the COVID crisis.

00:18:24.690 --> 00:18:25.705
And what we saw and

00:18:25.705 --> 00:18:31.040
the role that I had to take as a leader
was one of authenticity and vulnerability.

00:18:31.040 --> 00:18:32.802
We did not have the answers,

00:18:32.802 --> 00:18:36.740
and I was actually in charge of
helping a lot of Mexico CEOs.

00:18:36.740 --> 00:18:40.487
And a lot of our clients and advertisers
work through that journey of showing up

00:18:40.487 --> 00:18:42.950
authentically, showing
up with vulnerability.

00:18:42.950 --> 00:18:46.659
I mean, and we still do have
people struggling with health,

00:18:46.659 --> 00:18:48.854
mental health issues and whatnot.

00:18:48.854 --> 00:18:51.372
And, yes, we are driving for
performance, but

00:18:51.372 --> 00:18:55.181
caring of the individual as a whole has
been paramount to all of the efforts

00:18:55.181 --> 00:18:57.900
that we've been doing and
that I learned at the GSB.

00:18:57.900 --> 00:19:01.285
Like I couldn't have done that without
the tools that I learned at the GSB.

00:19:08.263 --> 00:19:13.512
&gt;&gt; So I think prior to going to ten at
the GSB, I think my identity is like

00:19:13.512 --> 00:19:18.840
being Latino, being like first
gen was not really top of mind.

00:19:18.840 --> 00:19:22.305
Honestly it took a backseat
to a lot of other things for

00:19:22.305 --> 00:19:26.165
most of my undergrad in my
professional career before MBA.

00:19:26.165 --> 00:19:31.618
But arriving at Stanford, and I think, and
you'd see this in any kind of top business

00:19:31.618 --> 00:19:36.264
school, just seeing that diversity
isn't really where it ought to be.

00:19:36.264 --> 00:19:40.898
I think that kind of helped me, figure out
and I realized how important that was to

00:19:40.898 --> 00:19:45.610
me and how much time I personally wanted
to dedicate to those issues like that.

00:19:45.610 --> 00:19:50.080
So I don't know specifically how it's
influenced my own personal leadership

00:19:50.080 --> 00:19:54.890
style, but it certainly has influenced
what I choose to invest my time in.

00:19:54.890 --> 00:19:58.190
And what it is that I feel
like strong conviction around,

00:19:58.190 --> 00:20:00.340
making sure that I raise my voice in.

00:20:01.550 --> 00:20:05.077
So I mean, in addition to the first gen
income stuff, I was co-president for

00:20:05.077 --> 00:20:08.022
the Hispanic Business student
Association while at Stanford.

00:20:08.022 --> 00:20:12.955
But I also kind of the idea of
helping underserved communities was

00:20:12.955 --> 00:20:17.715
always kind of a common theme in
my own personal career search.

00:20:17.715 --> 00:20:20.562
And I'm pretty fortunate that I've
been able to land somewhere where I

00:20:20.562 --> 00:20:22.562
feel that my job is
consistent with those values.

00:20:22.562 --> 00:20:24.992
Even though I am working
at a VC backed startup.

00:20:24.992 --> 00:20:28.110
I think it's pretty rare
that someone can say that.

00:20:28.110 --> 00:20:32.833
So I think that it's, the perspective
I got from Stanford through that

00:20:32.833 --> 00:20:37.792
kind of fueled my career search but also
kind of the way I look at the world and

00:20:37.792 --> 00:20:39.857
how I want to operate within it.

00:20:39.857 --> 00:20:42.933
&gt;&gt; 100% on that 100, like I couldn't have,

00:20:42.933 --> 00:20:47.046
said that even better try to think
of an eloquent way to state that.

00:20:47.046 --> 00:20:51.526
But I think my experience of
the GSB was one that magnified my

00:20:51.526 --> 00:20:55.565
ability to realize,
my gosh, I'm a black woman.

00:20:55.565 --> 00:21:00.505
And there's so much here that we
have to continue to press for or

00:21:00.505 --> 00:21:05.087
toward if want to see more of
me at this school, in society.

00:21:05.087 --> 00:21:10.440
And I'm using more of me, but I mean,
just like professional women,

00:21:10.440 --> 00:21:13.890
professional black people at the table,
and

00:21:13.890 --> 00:21:17.183
I have a strong positioning in doing that.

00:21:17.183 --> 00:21:22.044
And so, that's what really I think
it kind of changed my calculus of

00:21:22.044 --> 00:21:25.458
my career was thinking
about the opportunity as

00:21:25.458 --> 00:21:29.810
a capital allocator to be able
to pour money into underserved

00:21:29.810 --> 00:21:34.792
communities as a way of being able
to back underserved individuals.

00:21:34.792 --> 00:21:40.167
And that being a catalyst into the trickle
down effect of who is employed and

00:21:40.167 --> 00:21:41.716
who is making money.

00:21:41.716 --> 00:21:47.693
And so it just really changed my
mindset in terms of my positioning and

00:21:47.693 --> 00:21:49.969
the work that I want to do.

00:21:49.969 --> 00:21:54.636
And how I want to serve communities and so
now my work I'm specifically focused on,

00:21:54.636 --> 00:21:58.318
how can I invest in thinking about
diversity as a value enhancer?

00:21:58.318 --> 00:22:02.847
So not a concessionary return type
of thing, but to say diversity,

00:22:02.847 --> 00:22:05.000
the thing that's here to stay.

00:22:05.000 --> 00:22:07.050
And I think that there's
a real value in that.

00:22:07.050 --> 00:22:10.760
And so
how do I back diverse owned companies?

00:22:10.760 --> 00:22:14.684
How do I put diverse people
into leadership positions and

00:22:14.684 --> 00:22:16.410
governance positions.

00:22:16.410 --> 00:22:19.053
And then think about the underlying
societies that they're serving.

00:22:19.053 --> 00:22:25.550
So yeah, it's a huge push and
change of direction for me.

00:22:28.113 --> 00:22:32.774
&gt;&gt; Awesome, thank you all for
sharing those reflections and

00:22:32.774 --> 00:22:37.922
I feel like you all bring up
something that I want to acknowledge.

00:22:37.922 --> 00:22:42.847
We're not a perfect institution,
we have work to do when it

00:22:42.847 --> 00:22:47.883
comes to diversity and
to representation and to inclusion.

00:22:47.883 --> 00:22:50.776
We're not perfect, and
that's something that I and

00:22:50.776 --> 00:22:54.320
I think many of my colleagues
would transparently say.

00:22:54.320 --> 00:23:00.030
One thing that I have admired during my
time at Stanford so far is that there's,

00:23:00.030 --> 00:23:05.504
I think institutionally a will to change
and a responsiveness to students.

00:23:05.504 --> 00:23:10.358
For example,
last summer as a response to the uprisings

00:23:10.358 --> 00:23:14.696
against murders of black
folks by police forces,

00:23:14.696 --> 00:23:19.863
Stanford GSB launched the action plan for
racial equity and

00:23:19.863 --> 00:23:26.081
it's something that we're trying
to hold ourselves accountable to.

00:23:26.081 --> 00:23:31.673
In the admissions office we are building
out partnerships with organizations

00:23:31.673 --> 00:23:37.701
that support folks from underrepresented
or historically excluded backgrounds.

00:23:37.701 --> 00:23:42.100
Such as Forte and
Management Leadership for Tomorrow.

00:23:42.100 --> 00:23:45.600
So we're not a perfect
institution by any means.

00:23:45.600 --> 00:23:50.209
But we are working to Be better.

00:23:50.209 --> 00:23:54.572
And that's something that I do admire
about our community and our institution.

00:23:54.572 --> 00:23:56.942
And I want to thank our panelists for

00:23:56.942 --> 00:24:01.437
their transparency around that
because they know some of the best

00:24:01.437 --> 00:24:06.108
purpose of these panels is to tell
the truth and and study at the GSB.

00:24:06.108 --> 00:24:09.141
So I appreciate that from you all as well.

00:24:09.141 --> 00:24:13.164
So I'm turning to our Q&amp;A box, and

00:24:13.164 --> 00:24:21.091
there are a couple questions I'm going to
see if I can kind of group them.

00:24:23.906 --> 00:24:29.222
Well, there are a couple of questions
about sort of transitions and

00:24:29.222 --> 00:24:34.552
the ways that the GSB might have
changed you, or changed your path.

00:24:34.552 --> 00:24:39.436
So for example, Charles asks,
how has your what matters

00:24:39.436 --> 00:24:43.935
most changed during and
after Stanford, if at all?

00:24:43.935 --> 00:24:47.412
I think it's such an interesting question.

00:24:47.412 --> 00:24:53.313
So I can say that again, how has your
what matters most changed during and

00:24:53.313 --> 00:24:55.648
after Stanford, if at all?

00:24:55.648 --> 00:24:57.395
&gt;&gt; It's a constant for me,

00:24:57.395 --> 00:25:01.817
I think I just mostly write about
like my family and the legacy.

00:25:01.817 --> 00:25:07.473
I feel like I'm creating and
that be opportunity or to take part of.

00:25:07.473 --> 00:25:12.212
But yeah, what it boils down to is my
family and how much they mean to me and

00:25:12.212 --> 00:25:13.622
how much it matters.

00:25:13.622 --> 00:25:18.141
And so that's a constant,
it's my number one most

00:25:18.141 --> 00:25:23.511
important thing value or
whatever, so that won't change.

00:25:26.268 --> 00:25:31.052
&gt;&gt; Yeah, my what matters most to me and
why when I wrote my essay was basically

00:25:31.052 --> 00:25:35.779
being a role model to younger
generation and underserved communities.

00:25:35.779 --> 00:25:39.561
When I wrote that I had in mind basically
my nephews like other kids growing up in

00:25:39.561 --> 00:25:42.329
my in my neighborhood,
I don't need to change too much.

00:25:42.329 --> 00:25:47.283
I think the way that I would kind of
reframe it is like being a change agent to

00:25:47.283 --> 00:25:49.328
support those individuals.

00:25:49.328 --> 00:25:53.688
Now, I think that's more top of mind
now than it was before the GSB.

00:25:53.688 --> 00:25:57.546
&gt;&gt; And I think that I mean,
you're listening to Alejandro and

00:25:57.546 --> 00:26:02.110
Jessica, if you really write it right,
it doesn't change, right?

00:26:02.110 --> 00:26:07.050
And more than changing permits being
a North Star when I left the GSB,

00:26:07.050 --> 00:26:09.698
I joined consulting in the Bay Area.

00:26:09.698 --> 00:26:14.469
And when I read it,
I was miserable in their role.

00:26:14.469 --> 00:26:16.689
And it took me like one
night of reading it and

00:26:16.689 --> 00:26:20.529
knowing that I needed to transition that
all of the things that I had built and

00:26:20.529 --> 00:26:24.309
what I wanted to do and what was my
mission was not getting fulfilled there.

00:26:24.309 --> 00:26:28.835
And so that's the reason why I decided
to go back to Mexico and then look for

00:26:28.835 --> 00:26:33.362
opportunities where I could have
the impact that I wanted to have which was

00:26:33.362 --> 00:26:35.930
Part of my whole what matters most to you.

00:26:35.930 --> 00:26:39.967
And why so I think it's more for
North Star and in my change in phones and

00:26:39.967 --> 00:26:43.739
whatever but if it's written correctly,
it should not change.

00:26:46.849 --> 00:26:50.921
&gt;&gt; I love that, and I feel like I hear
that so often from our students and

00:26:50.921 --> 00:26:54.644
from our alums about going
through the application process.

00:26:54.644 --> 00:26:58.482
That often the very first step is
reflecting, going through that

00:26:58.482 --> 00:27:02.668
process of introspection to really,
really figure out where you are,

00:27:02.668 --> 00:27:06.809
where you want to go with your career,
what matters most to you and why.

00:27:06.809 --> 00:27:11.077
And I feel like you capture that really,
really perfectly,

00:27:11.077 --> 00:27:14.758
sort of on the,
I think if what matters most to you and

00:27:14.758 --> 00:27:18.879
why is on the personal side on
the more professional side.

00:27:18.879 --> 00:27:24.098
We do have a couple questions too
about career pivots, Nate says

00:27:24.098 --> 00:27:30.199
I noticed Adriana and Ollie hundred
transitioned into the tech industry.

00:27:30.199 --> 00:27:34.970
Could you share a specific experience
like a class club internship, etc,

00:27:34.970 --> 00:27:39.979
at Stanford that had the largest impact
to prepare you for your current roles?

00:27:39.979 --> 00:27:44.147
So I think that's a question that
all of you could could respond

00:27:44.147 --> 00:27:49.103
to what's a class club internship that
Have the largest impact on you, and

00:27:49.103 --> 00:27:51.479
prepare you for your current role.

00:27:51.479 --> 00:27:55.585
Especially if you were pivoting,
which I believe actually all

00:27:55.585 --> 00:27:59.549
three of you were pivoting into
into different industries.

00:27:59.549 --> 00:28:00.719
So yeah, let's start there.

00:28:05.909 --> 00:28:09.513
&gt;&gt; I can start, so, for me,
when I was coming into the GSB,

00:28:09.513 --> 00:28:14.569
I had kind of said yeah, that I wanted to
work in autonomous vehicle technology.

00:28:14.569 --> 00:28:17.851
That was largely informed by coming
from the insurance industry,

00:28:17.851 --> 00:28:20.379
with wanting some way of
transitioning into tech.

00:28:20.379 --> 00:28:24.956
That eventually kind of evolved and
turned into, labor marketplaces for

00:28:24.956 --> 00:28:28.870
underserved communities,
specifically low wage workers.

00:28:28.870 --> 00:28:32.153
I think the things the specific
classes that helped me get there,

00:28:32.153 --> 00:28:35.150
it wasn't like a linear path,
but to help me get there was.

00:28:35.150 --> 00:28:37.796
One was startup crush which
I'm sure many viewers

00:28:37.796 --> 00:28:41.709
are familiar with the kind of the content
that's on the Stanford website.

00:28:41.709 --> 00:28:45.117
And the other was data and
decisions, which is pretty random,

00:28:45.117 --> 00:28:49.700
I don't think many people would attribute
any kind of like grouping from that class.

00:28:49.700 --> 00:28:53.615
But I did get to work on a project with
a startup that was a marketplace and

00:28:53.615 --> 00:28:58.117
that was the very first time I'd ever kind
of had first hand exposure with that type

00:28:58.117 --> 00:28:59.240
of business model.

00:28:59.240 --> 00:29:02.210
And that kind of got me really excited and

00:29:02.210 --> 00:29:05.454
I ended up wanting to
go in that direction.

00:29:09.025 --> 00:29:13.408
&gt;&gt; I can tell a little bit about
mine I ended up in tech by accident,

00:29:13.408 --> 00:29:17.154
my career has not been as
planned as other people's.

00:29:17.154 --> 00:29:21.245
But I think,
I'll talk about international and

00:29:21.245 --> 00:29:27.051
I've worked in the US, I worked in
Mexico and I'm back in the US now.

00:29:27.051 --> 00:29:31.099
And I think that gives you also
the very different perspective.

00:29:31.099 --> 00:29:36.002
So when I moved back to Mexico,
what I wanted to do is maximize impact and

00:29:36.002 --> 00:29:40.111
working to generate economic
empowerment in the country.

00:29:40.111 --> 00:29:44.555
And so tech was an easy choice in that
regard because when I was offered a job at

00:29:44.555 --> 00:29:48.526
PayPal it was to manage SMBs in
empowering digital payments for us.

00:29:48.526 --> 00:29:53.849
And basically you can see the chain of
impacts why they didn't join because

00:29:53.849 --> 00:29:59.024
it was the UK I joined because it was
the most scalable way to have impact.

00:29:59.024 --> 00:30:03.581
And I joined in a commercial
function where particularly for

00:30:03.581 --> 00:30:08.046
those who want to do tech abroad,
mostly outside of London,

00:30:08.046 --> 00:30:12.422
Switzerland, Tel Aviv and
maybe a layover in Singapore,

00:30:12.422 --> 00:30:15.839
most of the tech roles
tend to be commercial.

00:30:15.839 --> 00:30:19.013
This would you develop a really
strong commercial acumen.

00:30:19.013 --> 00:30:20.953
And so that's going back to the US,

00:30:20.953 --> 00:30:25.495
I'm more focused on learning about product
which I haven't learned in my career.

00:30:25.495 --> 00:30:30.266
But expanding that product components to
my skill set that is mostly commercial

00:30:30.266 --> 00:30:32.199
on me the partnerships things.

00:30:32.199 --> 00:30:35.129
I think classes specifically it's hard,
I mean of course,

00:30:35.129 --> 00:30:37.259
you're going to have to use data and
whatnot.

00:30:37.259 --> 00:30:40.485
But for me,
there's two critical classes one is and

00:30:40.485 --> 00:30:44.240
I don't know if they still exist,
out almost ten years now.

00:30:44.240 --> 00:30:46.753
It's got critical analytical thinking,

00:30:46.753 --> 00:30:49.683
the biggest gap will see
in talent particularly in

00:30:49.683 --> 00:30:54.235
emerging markets is critical and
analytical thinking when it comes to tech.

00:30:54.235 --> 00:30:58.742
Data is now pervasive and it's how you
use that data, how you question, and

00:30:58.742 --> 00:31:03.189
how do you do hypotheses or hypothesis
testing that's really important.

00:31:03.189 --> 00:31:07.426
And the other one is your Peterson's
managing growing enterprises,

00:31:07.426 --> 00:31:12.016
I use it every day is as a people leader
and as a people manager that can country

00:31:12.016 --> 00:31:16.139
cases that you review and
the skill sets that you develop on people.

00:31:16.139 --> 00:31:21.156
Literally, there's not a day where I
don't put some of that in practice.

00:31:21.156 --> 00:31:24.716
&gt;&gt; Yeah, now there's a second
on a couple of different things,

